course: dictionary
lesson: 3210
title: Making nouns with -ment
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Enjoyment is the feeling of pleasure you get when you like doing something. It is a noun, a word for a thing or feeling, and it is uncountable, so we do not say "two enjoyments".
explain: Look at the ending. Enjoy is a verb, a doing word. Add -ment and you get enjoyment, a noun. The ending -ment makes a thing from a verb, just as move becomes movement.
explain: Read this in context: «Reading for enjoyment» means reading because you like it, not because you have to. In «coaching, rest and enjoyment matter too», enjoyment is one of the things that helps you practise well. Check by swapping in your meaning: "feeling of pleasure" fits, so you have it right.
fact: Enjoyment is a noun, and it is uncountable.
fact: The ending -ment makes a thing from a verb: enjoy becomes enjoyment, and move becomes movement.
fact: Endings that start with a consonant, such as -ment, usually do not change the root word, so the spelling stays enjoy + ment.
fact: «Enjoyment» means the feeling of pleasure you get when you like doing something.
fact: The «10,000 hours» idea says practice matters a lot, but there is no magic number, and «coaching, rest and enjoyment matter too.»
exercise: «She read the book for enjoyment, not for a test.» What does enjoyment mean here? | A test with lots of questions | A library card | Being bored and tired of reading | *The feeling of pleasure from doing something you like
exercise: Which ending makes a noun from a verb, as in enjoy to enjoyment? | -ful | *-ment | -less | -ly
exercise: Add -ment to move. Which word do you make? | mover | *movement | moveing | moved
test: «Coaching, rest and enjoyment matter too.» What part of speech is enjoyment in this sentence? | Adverb | Verb | Adjective | *Noun
test: Mia says enjoyment and enjoy are the same word. What is the difference? | Enjoyment is only used for sport | Enjoyment is a verb, and enjoy is a noun | They are both nouns, so there is no difference | *Enjoy is a verb, a doing word, and enjoyment is a noun, the feeling or thing
exam: Which sentence uses enjoyment correctly? | They enjoymented the game. | They enjoyment the game. | *They had great enjoyment playing the game. | They are enjoyment the game.
